Azure VM Image (Equivalent to AMI in AWS)
An Azure Managed Image or Shared Image is a template that contains the OS, data disks, and pre-installed applications, used to create new VMs with consistent configurations.

What an Azure VM Image Contains:
          Operating system (Windows/Linux)
          
          Configuration (custom software, settings, patches)
          
          OS disk + optional data disks

Types of Images in Azure
          Managed Image : A single-region, account-specific image. Created from a generalized or specialized VM. Good for limited reuse.
          
          Shared Image Gallery (SIG) : For distributing images across multiple regions. Supports versioning and scaling VM deployments.
          
          Marketplace Images : Prebuilt solutions provided by Microsoft or partners (SQL, Ubuntu, RHEL, etc.).

Use Cases for Each:
          Managed Image: Internal testing, backup of a specific machine.
          
          SIG (Shared Image Gallery): Enterprise-wide reuse, replication across regions.
          
          Marketplace: Quick setup of vendor solutions (e.g., WordPress, Jenkins, etc.).


Azure Equivalent Steps (Same as AMI)
 
Step 1: Prepare Your VM
          Create a Windows VM (e.g., VM-server-japan)
          Attach extra disk if needed
          Log in via RDP, create sample files/folders for testing
          Ensure the VM is set up as required

Step 2: Deallocate and Generalize the VM
          ~ Required to create a generalized image

            Stop (Deallocate) the VM
            State should be: Stopped (deallocated)

Wait for VM to shut down automatically

Step 3: Create the Image
            Go to Azure Portal > Virtual Machines
            Select your stopped VM
            Click Capture
            Choose: Name for the Image
            Resource group
            Option: “Automatically delete this VM after creating the image” – optional
            Image Type: Generalized (after Sysprep)
            Specialized (skip Sysprep)
            Click Create

Step 4: Use the Image to Launch New VMs
            Go to Images in Azure
            Select the image
            Click Create VM
            Configure networking, disk, name, etc.
            Skip admin credentials if using a specialized image


